================================================================================
Where the facts come from
================================================================================

Every rule states a fact about how a server, a class file or a pack behaves, and
each one was established from a primary source rather than from recollection.
The source is recorded on the rule and printed by `pluginscope rules` and by
`--verbose`, so any claim the tool makes can be checked.

  Libraries the server provides to plugins
    Read from the published POM of io.papermc.paper:paper-api
    (26.2.build.116-stable) on repo.papermc.io. Only compile-scope entries are
    listed, because those are the ones a plugin sees. The server's own runtime
    dependencies are deliberately not in the table: whether a plugin classloader
    can reach them is a separate question this project has not verified.

  api-version numbering
    Read from apiVersioning.json inside the paper-api jar, which carries
    {"version":"26.2.build.116-stable","currentApiVersion":"26.2"}. The accepted
    shapes (major.minor[.patch], or the literal "none") follow Paper's own
    parsing in CraftMagicNumbers / ApiVersion.

  Plugin descriptor keys and the plugin-name pattern
    Read out of the constant pool of org/bukkit/plugin/PluginDescriptionFile.class
    in the same paper-api jar, including the VALID_NAME pattern ^[A-Za-z0-9 _.-]+$
    and the refusal of a main class inside the org.bukkit namespace.

  Class file major versions
    Fixed by the JVM specification: major 52 is Java 8 and each release adds one.

  Resource-pack format numbers
    Fetched at run time from the version summary published by misode/mcmeta and
    cached locally. That repository carries no licence file, so no copy of the
    data is vendored here: the tool downloads it when asked to and works fully
    offline when the format number is supplied directly.

  Pack metadata structure
    Field names, optionality and the supported_formats / min_format rules were
    taken from SpyglassMC's vanilla-mcdoc (MIT), which is a schema description
    rather than game data.

  Model Context Protocol
    Message shapes, the stdio framing rule and the error-code table were taken
    from the specification repository at modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol.

No Minecraft, Mojang, Paper, CraftBukkit or Bukkit code is included in, derived
into, or distributed with this project, and no game data is bundled. The rule
table quotes short identifiers and diagnostic strings so that its claims are
auditable against their sources.

